<DocsLayout title="Code orientation in Haft v9" description="How the fused code and reasoning graph supports bounded orientation without turning rank or traversal into authority.">
  <h1>Code orientation</h1>

  <p>
    Haft v9 fuses code symbols, calls, files, modules, decisions, invariants,
    notes, and other reasoning records into one query surface. The graph helps
    an agent find the relevant area before editing. It does not decide what the
    code means or which record governs it.
  </p>

  <h2>Explore an exact symbol or a concern</h2>

  <pre><code>haft graph explore --symbol PublishIndexEpoch --view working --json
haft graph explore --query "where is the index epoch published?" --view working --json</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>explore</code> accepts one input shape at a time: an exact symbol or a
    bounded concern. The concern route returns advisory candidates. The caller
    must select an exact code identity from the current use, not from rank
    alone.
  </p>

  <p>The equivalent MCP calls are:</p>

  <pre><code>haft_query(action="explore", symbol="PublishIndexEpoch")
haft_query(action="explore", query="where is the index epoch published?")</code></pre>

  <h2>Use the actual target before editing</h2>

  <p>
    Area exploration is not an edit preflight. Before a non-mechanical change
    where recorded governance may matter, query the actual file or symbol:
  </p>

  <pre><code>haft_query(action="code_context", file="internal/index/epoch.go")
haft_query(action="impact", symbol="PublishIndexEpoch")
haft_query(action="governing_set", source_refs=["symbol:PublishIndexEpoch"])</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>code_context</code> and <code>impact</code> are relevance surfaces. A
    displayed invariant, a nearby decision, or module proximity is not by
    itself proof of active authority. Inspect the exact record's scope, status,
    coverage, and limiting reasons before relying on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Views and replay</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>View</th>
        <th>Use</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>working</code></td>
        <td>Bounded, score-free orientation without retrieval internals</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>trace</code></td>
        <td>Bounded provenance plus an opaque replay basis</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>diagnostic</code></td>
        <td>Retrieval and traversal internals for debugging the query</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Replay is tied to the same request and source snapshot. If the index,
    request, or canonical result changes, Haft returns
    <code>replay_mismatch</code> instead of pretending the old trace still
    proves the new result.
  </p>

  <h2>Freshness under concurrent hosts</h2>

  <p>
    Code indexing is automatic and shared by concurrent <code>haft serve</code>
    processes for the same project. One process publishes a changed epoch;
    followers recheck the completed publication. A bounded request that cannot
    establish freshness reports a retained complete epoch as degraded, or
    reports the index unavailable when no complete epoch exists.
  </p>

  <p>
    Several running project servers are not a defect by count alone. Freshness
    and exact project binding matter more than process count.
  </p>

  <h2>Boundaries that prevent false confidence</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>An empty caller or callee list is not proof that no dependency exists.</li>
    <li>Incomplete traversal is not a safety claim.</li>
    <li>A coverage cue is not proof that every file is governed.</li>
    <li>A missing exact link is not proof that the file is unconstrained.</li>
    <li>FPF identifiers, Haft record IDs, project-memory identities, and code symbols remain separate namespaces.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Two orientation systems:</strong> the code graph answers where
      code and governing records may be related. Typed project memory answers
      what is known around one exact EntityOfConcern. Neither substitutes for
      the other.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Practical sequence</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Use <code>explore</code> when you do not yet know the exact symbol.</li>
    <li>Select the code identity from the current task, not candidate rank.</li>
    <li>Run <code>code_context</code> or <code>impact</code> on the actual target.</li>
    <li>Recover the exact governing set before relying on a governance claim.</li>
    <li>Record <code>not_applicable</code> for a purely mechanical edit instead of inventing ceremony.</li>
  </ol>
</DocsLayout>
